0 left), so not great for sequences. T1 and T3 are isolated, but T6-8 meld is already T suit, so maybe half flush with honors possible.  \nUnshown tiles: W1 (4 left), W3 (1 left), W4 (1), W6 (1), W7 (2), W8 (2), W9 (1), T1 (4), T3 (3), B2 (3), F1 (2), F2 (4), F3 (2) \u2014 many honors still available.  \n\nCurrent patterns potential:  \n- Could aim for **All Pungs** or **All Honors** if get pungs of F2, F1, F3, W1.  \n- Also **Half Flush** possible if I stick to W suit + honors (W1 triple, F1, F2, F3 pungs).  \nBut B2 pair is useless if going honors/half flush.  \n- **Seat Wind** is West (F3) \u2014 I have one F3, so getting pung of F3 would give 2 points.  \n- **Prevalent Wind** is South (F2) \u2014 I have two F2, good chance for pung.  \n\nLooking at discard options:  \nT1, T3, B2, F1, F2, F3, W1.  \n\nIf I discard T1 or T3 or B2, I move toward honor-based hand.  \nIf I discard W1, I lose a potential pung (and W1 is terminal, could give Pung of Terminals points).  \n\nGiven the low remaining W3/W4 etc., it\u2019s hard to get W sequences.  \n\nBest discard to improve hand flexibility and scoring: Keep the 3 W1 (ready pung) and F2 pair (prevailing wind) and F1/F3 singles (seat wind possible). Remove a suited tile that\u2019s hard to use.  \n\nAmong suited tiles: T1 and T3 are isolated, T1 has 4 left but T2 only 3 left, T3 also isolated, but T6-8 meld means maybe keep T suit? Not really, since T1/T3 far from T6-8. Better to discard T1 or T3. T1 is a terminal, keeping it might allow Pung of Terminals if drawn two more, but T1 has 4 left, so possible but needs two draws \u2014 unlikely soon. T3 has 3 left, same logic. B2 pair is useless unless going for mixed hand; I\u2019d rather break B2 pair to keep honors.  \n\nBut if I discard B2, I lose a pair; then I need to form another pair from honors. I have F2 pair already, so safe to discard one B2. But discarding F1, F2, or F3 would break potential honor pungs.  \n\nF1 has 2 left unshown, so pung possible if I get two more draws; F3 same.  \n\nActually, W1 triple is a locked terminal pung already (3 of them), so that\u2019s a guaranteed 1+ point (Pung of Terminals).  \n\nSo most promising: Keep W1, F2 pair, F1, F3, and try to draw more honors to complete pungs. So discard a suited tile.  \n\nBetween T1, T3, B2: B2 is the only one not terminal, so discarding B2 retains more chance for \"All Terminals and Honors\" (32 points) if I later get rid of T1/T3 too \u2014 but that\u2019s very high-point pattern but needs all terminals/honors. Given my hand already has W1 (terminal), T1 (terminal), T3 (not terminal), B2 (not terminal), it\u2019s not all terminals/honors yet.  \n\nGiven current state, safer to go for **Half Flush** (W suit + honors) or **All Honors**. All Honors requires ditching all suited tiles, so I should discard T1, T3, B2 eventually. Discarding B2 now is okay but B2 is easier to get rid of later since not terminal. T1 and T3 are terminals; discarding them early loses Pung of Terminals chance for them.  \n\nBetter discard: B2 \u2014 because:  \n1. It\u2019s not a terminal, so not useful for All Terminals and Honors now.  \n2. It\u2019s not W suit, so not useful for Half Flush (W + honors).  \n3. Breaking B2 pair frees tiles to draw honors.  \n4. Keeping T1 and T3 leaves open Pung of Terminals chances (each could become pung if drawn more, giving extra points).  \n\nBut also opponents\u2019 discards: Prev player discarded T2, J3, B6 etc.; Next player discarded F3, W5, etc. F3 is seat wind, maybe they avoid it; but I have F3, so maybe they\u2019ll discard more.  \n\nLooking at unshown counts: F2 has 4 left, F1 has 2, F3 has 2, W1 has 4 \u2014 many remain. Good chance to draw them.  \n\n**Conclusion:** Discard B2 now to move toward honors/W1-based hand.  \n\n**Reason:** B2 is the only non-terminal, non-W suit tile in my hand (besides T1/T3, which are terminals and could yield Pung of Terminals points). Discarding B2 keeps the option for Half Flush (W + honors) and All Honors patterns, while preserving possible Pung of Terminals from T1 or T3 if drawn later. It also maintains the F2 pair (prevailing wind) and F1/F3 for seat/wind pungs. This maximizes scoring potential given visible tiles and opponents\u2019 melds which show they are collecting suited sequences, leaving honors possibly easier to draw.  \n\n**Answer:** Play B2"}
